---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Install = `pip install rich`;

const Colors = `from rich import print

print("[bold red]Danger![/bold red] The dragon noticed you.")
print("[green]You found 12 gold.[/green]")
print("[bold cyan]===== INVENTORY =====[/bold cyan]")`;

const Console = `from rich.console import Console

console = Console()

console.print("Welcome to the shop!", style="bold magenta")
console.print("Sold out", style="dim")`;

const Table = `from rich.console import Console
from rich.table import Table

console = Console()

table = Table(title="High Scores")
table.add_column("Player")
table.add_column("Score", justify="right")

table.add_row("Ana", "1200")
table.add_row("Ben", "980")
table.add_row("Cal", "870")

console.print(table)`;

const Panel = `from rich.console import Console
from rich.panel import Panel

console = Console()

console.print(Panel("You have entered the cave.", title="Chapter 2"))`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Library Exploration: Rich</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Optional &mdash; Extra Credit</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Rich</b> makes terminal output look good. Color, bold text, tables,
            boxes &mdash; all the things plain <code>print()</code> cannot do.
          </p>

          <p>
            This one is deliberately placed right before <b>Group Project 1</b>.
            Your project is a terminal application, and the difference between a
            wall of gray text and something with a colored header and a real
            scoreboard is enormous for about ten minutes of work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nothing here changes how your program <i>works</i>. It changes how it
            looks. Get the program working first, then come back and make it
            pretty.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="setup">
          <h2>Installing It</h2>

          <p>
            Rich does not come with Python &mdash; unlike <code>turtle</code> or{" "}
            <code>math</code>, you have to install it:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Install}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="bash"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>If that fails with a permissions error</b>, the school machines may
            not let you install packages. Come and tell me rather than fighting it
            &mdash; there is a way around it, and it is not your problem to solve.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="explore">
          <h2>Work Through These</h2>

          <h4>1. Color, the easy way</h4>

          <p>
            Import Rich's <code>print</code> instead of the built-in one and you
            can put style tags straight in your text. Everything else about{" "}
            <code>print()</code> works exactly as before.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Colors}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-rich.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The tags come in pairs, like <code>[green]</code> and{" "}
            <code>[/green]</code> &mdash; the one with the slash turns the style
            back off. Try <b>red</b>, <b>green</b>, <b>yellow</b>, <b>blue</b>,{" "}
            <b>magenta</b>, <b>cyan</b>, and combinations like{" "}
            <code>[bold underline red]</code>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h4>2. A Console object</h4>

          <p>
            The other way to do it: make a <code>Console</code> and pass a style
            separately. Useful when the same style gets reused a lot.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Console}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-rich.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h4>3. A real table</h4>

          <p>
            This is the one worth learning. Remember the Kg to Lbs table, where
            lining up the columns by hand was the fiddly part? Rich does that for
            you.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Table}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-rich.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Notice <code>add_row</code> takes <b>strings</b>, not numbers &mdash;
            so if your scores are integers, you will need to convert them on the
            way in. Which is casting, in the opposite direction from Activity 1.8.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h4>4. Panels</h4>

          <Code
            code={Panel}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-rich.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Good for chapter headings in a story game, or for framing a menu.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="make">
          <h2>Then Improve Something You Already Wrote</h2>

          <p>
            Do not start a new program for this. Go back to one of your finished
            code challenges and make it look better:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Give your <b>Dungeon Crawler</b> colored text for danger and
              treasure, and a panel for each chapter
            </li>
            <li>
              Turn <b>Heads or Tails</b>' win/loss score into a real table
            </li>
            <li>
              Color your <b>Traffic Light</b> output to match the actual light
            </li>
            <li>
              Give your <b>Playlist Builder</b> a table with track numbers in one
              column and titles in the other
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>To submit:</b> the before and after &mdash; your original file and
            the Rich version, so the difference is visible.
          </p>

          <p>
            And keep it in mind for the group project. A terminal app with a
            colored header and a clean table reads as finished in a way that plain
            text does not.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Go Make It Look Good
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
